options.description:
Only worth it if R12 shows VISIONOS URLs carry `n`. Then:
- load the cipher script in parallel with the first player POST;
- persist the extracted PlayerScript (the few-KB signature and `n` functions) per player id, so later launches skip the regex over base.js;
- cache a failed download for ~60 s;
- keep one JSContext per script with the functions defined once.
Saves 0.1-0.6 s on a cold launch when deciphering is needed, and 5-30 ms per resolve. Risk: low. Effort: M.

Replace session.data(for:) with a URLSessionDataDelegate session:
- check the headers when the response arrives;
- write and answer AVPlayer in ~64 KiB slices as data arrives;
- share one download per video, so concurrent loading requests wait on bytes in flight instead of fetching them again.
This is Android's 64 KB streaming. Saves 50-300 ms more than R2 on slow links, and also speeds seeks into uncached parts. Risk: medium. Effort: L.

Add a start-timings log and signposts, plus a 'Last start' breakdown at the end of the deep-probe report. It records:
- tap -> resolved URL -> loadTracks -> first .playing;
- resolve time with strategy, remote-config wait, and whether the URL had `n`;
- loader-request arrivals (offset/length/toEnd/content-info) and cancellations;
- first network byte and first respond(with:).
Saves nothing by itself. Risk: none. Effort: S.

recommendedPlan.change:
Two request settings plus the retry rules.
- Thread `allowsConstrained: Bool = true` through prefetch() -> data() -> fetch() and set `request.allowsConstrainedNetworkAccess` (false only for speculative prefetch).
- R6: set `request.assumesHTTP3Capable = true` when `URLCoding.host(stream.url)` ends with `.googlevideo.com`.
- R5a retry policy, porting Android oct3 CloudStreamProxy.kt:253-279:
  - maxResolutions 3 -> 4;
  - the first 401/403/404/410 for a strategy invalidates and re-resolves with the same exclusions;
  - a second failure from the same strategy inserts it into `excluded`, as today;
  - 429/500/502/503/504: `try await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(250 * attempt))`, then retry (cancellation propagates);
  - the formatChanged check in store() stays.

R7. The helper must live in DualDeckEngine.swift, because it needs that file's private members: setPreparing :689, pendingStartSeconds :43, consecutiveFailures :47 and private(set) hasEnded.
- In jump(to:), before advance(): `if adoptPreparedIncoming(target: target, index: index) { return }`.
- The helper:
  - guard `fade == nil`, `let prepared = preparedIncoming`, `prepared.entry.id == target.id`, `idle.currentItem === prepared`, `activeItem != nil`;
  - `let incomingDeck = idle`, captured BEFORE swapping. `idle` is computed (:30); the old plan's `idle.restoreStallWaiting()` after `active = idle` would hit the outgoing deck;
  - cancelScheduledHandOver() (pauses, restores stall waiting and seeks 0 on the scheduled deck);
  - cancel and clear crossfadeTask and preparingIncomingTask; set plannedTransition = nil;
  - `prepared.tap.ramp.publish(nil)`, because a crossfade incoming ramp starts at 0 gain (Crossfade.swift:143-146);
  - `let previous = activeItem`; `discard(active.removeAll())`; `active = incomingDeck`; `activeItem = prepared`; `preparedIncoming = nil`;
  - `incomingDeck.restoreStallWaiting()`; if prepared.positionSeconds > 0.05, `incomingDeck.seek(to: 0)`;
  - hasEnded = false; pendingStartSeconds = 0; consecutiveFailures = 0;
  - queue.setCurrentIndex(index); emit(.currentIndexChanged(index)); onItemTransition?(prepared.song, previous?.song, false);
  - if playWhenReady { startActive() }; setPreparing(false); onTimingChanged?(); scheduleNext(); return true.
- AppTests/DualDeckEngineTests.swift: with crossfade on, after the incoming item is prepared, skipToNext reuses it. Use a counting resolver wrapped around the factory's resolver: the count doesn't change, the active deck's current item is the prepared one, and its ramp is nil. Add a second case for a gapless hand-over inside the 4.5 s window.

R8: add init parameters `strategyTimeoutSeconds: Double = 15` and `hedgeAfterSeconds: Double? = nil`. With nil the behaviour is exactly today's, so the Android parity tests don't change. Otherwise resolveStream runs a withThrowingTaskGroup:
- start strategy 0; start strategy i+1 when strategy i fails or hedgeAfterSeconds has passed since it started (Task.sleep(nanoseconds:), as withTimeout does at :261);
- return the first non-blank URL and cancel the rest;
- record attempts in completion order; `validate` and `excludedStrategies` keep their current behaviour.
InnerTubeService passes `strategyTimeoutSeconds: 8, hedgeAfterSeconds: 1.5`. Add Swift Testing cases: a slow first strategy loses to a fast second; a failing first starts the second at once; nil means sequential. Record them in docs/test-parity.md as iOS-only. Note: concurrent strategies share InnerTubeClient.lastFailureReason (InnerTubeClient.swift:84, read at StreamResolution.swift:66), so a losing attempt's detail may be wrong (diagnostics only).

Persist the in-memory `cache` to Caches/YouTube/resolved/streams.json:
- use an app-side `nonisolated struct PersistedStream: Codable` (url, userAgent, strategyName, expiresAt), because PixlNet's ResolvedStream isn't Codable;
- load it lazily on the first resolve and drop entries expiring within 60 s;
- coalesce writes with one sleeping Task (2 s); skip Piped results; invalidate()/invalidateAll() remove entries too.
This needs no StreamCache.Meta change and no init reorder. (The old plan had the order backwards: YouTubeServices.swift creates the service at :56-57 and the cache at :59.)

Debounce meta persistence:
- write() updates `metas` and marks the video dirty;
- the JSON is saved when the file becomes complete, by one sleeping Task per dirty set (≤ 2 s, no ticking timer), and on remove();
- add `flush()`, called from evictIfNeeded and when the app goes to the background.
saveMeta already guards on `metas[videoId]`, so a pending save after remove() writes nothing. With R2's 3-4 extra writes per song this is not needed yet.

risks:
Prefetch v2 uses mobile data (about 0.5 MB per upcoming song, more at depth 2) and starts on-demand Spotify matching in the background, which means more YouTube search calls. Mitigations:
- Low Data Mode is honoured;
- work runs only while playing (Android's gate) and ≥ 3 s in;
- one task, cancelled on queue change and pause.

AGENTS rules:
- all warm-ups must be deferred and at utility priority (nothing heavy at launch);
- no change touches the processing-tap callback;
- timings use Synchronization.Mutex, or NSLock only in synchronous helpers (api-notes :115);
- PixlCore changes (hedging, fan-out matching, Codable PlayerScript) stay Foundation-only and Windows-buildable.
